# Write-permission guardrail — runs before every push (replaces the GitHub
# Action, which was flaky on GitHub's runner outages). The always-on CODE SCAN
# blocks a push that adds an undeclared write target (the real bug class); the
# DB-audit half runs only if Supabase creds are in the local env and is
# best-effort. This stops a bad push before it ever leaves the machine.
npm run audit:grants
# Affiliate hard-rule tripwire (James's #1): blocks a push that would ship a
# non-earning Amazon link — a raw amazon href outside the choke point, or a
# stored gear/book row on a foreign store / foreign tag. The DB half runs when
# Supabase creds are in the local env; the logic + component scan always run.
npm run audit:affiliate
# Schema-vs-code column guard (post-cleanup): blocks a push that writes a column
# to a core table which no committed migration creates — the exact class that
# took enrichment down (info_note) and left the geo_* fields unmigrated. No DB
# needed: the committed migrations are the schema of record; the guard is
# conservative and only fails on a column it's sure the code writes and the
# schema lacks.
npm run audit:schema
# Route-export guard: blocks a push where a Next.js route.ts exports anything other
# than an HTTP handler + segment config. `tsc` doesn't catch this, but `next build`
# fails on it in production — the exact class that failed two deploys (a test-only
# export left on the owner-pin route). No DB needed; a pure scan of app/**/route.ts.
npm run audit:routes
